        <p>
          There's a particular kind of grumbling that follows almost every
          school raffle: was the hat shuffled properly? Did the same three
          families always seem to win? Is there any record of who actually
          entered? Most of the time nothing dishonest happened — but a draw
          nobody could watch closely enough to trust leaves room for doubt, and
          doubt is corrosive to something that's supposed to be a fun, community
          moment.
        </p>
        <p>
          A free spin wheel, projected on a screen for everyone to see, removes
          that doubt almost entirely. It's visible from every seat in the hall.
          It's provably random rather than "probably fine." It builds genuine
          suspense as it slows down. And it costs nothing to run — no software,
          no printing, no login.
        </p>
        <p>
          This guide walks through the whole process — planning, setup, the live
          draw, and handling multiple prizes — so your next raffle is both fun
          to watch and impossible to argue with.
        </p>

