        <h2 id="why-wheel">1. Why a Spin Wheel Beats a Hat Full of Tickets</h2>
        <p>
          A hat draw has been the default for decades, but it has real,
          practical limits: only the person holding the hat can see the tickets
          get mixed, most of the room is too far away to actually watch anything
          happen, and there's no way to replay the moment if anyone questions it
          later.
        </p>
        <p>A spin wheel fixes each of those at once:</p>

        <ul class="uc-list">
          <li>
            <div class="uc-icon">👁️</div>
            <div class="uc-body">
              <strong>The whole room can see it</strong
              ><span
                >Project it on a hall screen and every student watches the same
                draw happen in real time — nobody's craning their neck to see a
                hand in a bag.</span
              >
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="uc-icon">🔒</div>
            <div class="uc-body">
              <strong>Nobody can tilt the result</strong
              ><span
                >The outcome is set by the browser the instant the spin starts,
                before the animation even plays — the person clicking has no way
                to influence where it lands. More on the mechanics in
                <a href="/blogs/is-spin-wheel-truly-random" class="ilink"
                  >is a spin wheel truly random</a
                >.</span
              >
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="uc-icon">🎊</div>
            <div class="uc-body">
              <strong>It's genuinely exciting to watch</strong
              ><span
                >The slowing tick as the wheel decelerates builds real suspense
                — a quiet, held-breath moment a hand reaching into a hat just
                doesn't produce.</span
              >
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="uc-icon">📋</div>
            <div class="uc-body">
              <strong>You can record it and prove it</strong
              ><span
                >Screen-record the draw and you've got a permanent record anyone
                can watch afterward — parents who missed it, students who want a
                replay, or anyone who has a question later.</span
              >
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="uc-icon">💰</div>
            <div class="uc-body">
              <strong>Costs nothing to run</strong
              ><span
                >No printed tickets, no box, no bag. Just a browser and your
                list of entrants — every pound the raffle raises stays in the
                fundraiser.</span
              >
            </div>
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="before">
          2. Three Things to Decide Before You Build the Wheel
        </h2>
        <p>Get these settled first and the setup itself takes minutes.</p>

        <h3>Who's entering, and how?</h3>
        <p>
          Decide whether it's open to the whole school, one year group, or
          families too. Then decide how entries are earned — bought tickets,
          merit points, event participation, or simply being present. This
          decides how you'll build the wheel.
        </p>
        <h3>What are the prizes, and in what order?</h3>
        <p>
          List prizes from smallest to biggest. Save the headline prize for last
          — it's what keeps the room's attention through every earlier spin.
        </p>
        <h3>Does everyone get equal odds?</h3>
        <p>
          A simple draw gives everyone one entry. A fundraising raffle with
          multiple tickets per family needs weighted entries instead — covered
          in Section 4.
        </p>

        <div class="box box-warn">
          <div class="box-label">⚠️ A note on regulations</div>
          <p>
            A raffle that involves selling tickets can fall under your country's
            gambling or lottery regulations — in the UK, for instance, that's
            typically the Gambling Act 2005. Check with your school's
            administration before running a paid-ticket raffle. This guide
            covers the draw mechanism, not local compliance.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h2 id="setup">3. Setting Up Your Raffle Wheel, Step by Step</h2>

        <ul class="step-list">
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Open the wheel on your draw device</strong>
              <p>
                Go to <a href="/" class="ilink">spinthewheelsonline.com</a> on
                whichever laptop will be connected to the projector on draw day
                — building and drawing on the same device saves you rebuilding
                it later.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Add every entrant's name or ticket number</strong>
              <p>
                Type or paste each name into the wheel. For numbered raffles,
                enter ticket numbers instead — the wheel works the same either
                way.
              </p>
              <ul>
                <li>Small draws: type names directly</li>
                <li>Larger draws: paste in bulk from a spreadsheet column</li>
                <li>
                  Double-check the count matches your confirmed entrant list
                </li>
              </ul>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Turn on "remove after spin" for multiple prizes</strong>
              <p>
                This drops each winner off the wheel automatically once they're
                drawn, so nobody can win twice in the same session.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Bookmark or save the wheel URL</strong>
              <p>
                This preserves your whole setup — names, settings, everything —
                so it reloads exactly as built, with nothing to re-enter on the
                day.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Test the projector display beforehand</strong>
              <p>
                Check fullscreen mode looks right on your hall screen, and
                confirm the ticking sound is audible — it's a big part of what
                makes the draw feel exciting. Do this the day before, not five
                minutes before.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Set up screen recording (worth doing)</strong>
              <p>
                Windows: Win + G. Mac: Cmd + Shift + 5. Start recording before
                you open the wheel so the full entry list is captured too.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="weighted">4. Handling Multi-Ticket and Weighted Entries</h2>
        <p>
          When families buy more than one ticket, each one should count as an
          equal extra chance. The fix is simple:
          <strong>enter a name once for every ticket they hold.</strong>
        </p>

        <div class="box box-amber">
          <div class="box-label">📋 Example</div>
          <p>
            The Ahmed family bought 5 tickets, the Ortiz family bought 1. Add
            "Ahmed" five times and "Ortiz" once. The odds now match the tickets
            sold exactly — the wheel's randomness handles the rest.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h3>Doing this at scale</h3>
        <p>
          For a large raffle, it's faster to enter ticket numbers instead of
          names. If you sold tickets 001–300, add all 300 numbers to the wheel,
          then match the winning number to your sales ledger. No repeated typing
          needed.
        </p>

        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table class="comp-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Raffle Type</th>
                <th>Wheel Entry Method</th>
                <th>Best For</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>🎯 Equal chance</td>
                <td>One name per student</td>
                <td>Classroom draws, attendance prizes, merit draws</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>🎟️ Multi-ticket, named</td>
                <td>Weight set via + button (1x–20x) or repeated entries</td>
                <td>Small named-ticket fundraisers</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>🔢 Numbered tickets</td>
                <td>All sold numbers entered</td>
                <td>Fetes, large fundraising raffles</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>⭐ Merit-weighted</td>
                <td>Scaled with + button per merit tier</td>
                <td>End-of-term reward draws</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h2 id="draw-day">5. Running the Live Draw Like an Event</h2>

        <ul class="step-list">
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Show the full entry list first</strong>
              <p>
                Scroll through the wheel before the first spin so everyone can
                see all entries are there. State the total count out loud for
                numbered raffles.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Let a student or parent press spin</strong>
              <p>
                Someone outside the organising team clicking the button removes
                any perception that the operator could be timing the result —
                and gives that person a nice moment in front of their peers.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Don't talk over the ticking</strong>
              <p>
                The slowing wheel is doing the engagement work on its own. Let
                the room sit with it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Announce the winner and give them a moment</strong>
              <p>
                Read the name or number clearly, repeat it, and pause. Don't
                rush straight to the next spin.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Show the removal, then move on</strong>
              <p>
                With remove-after-spin on, point out that the winning name has
                disappeared before spinning again — visible proof no one wins
                twice.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
        </ul>

        <figure class="ai-image">
          <img
            src="/bulk-names.webp"
            alt="A student excitedly pointing at a prize wheel that has just stopped spinning on a school hall screen, classmates cheering in the background, warm celebratory illustration style"
            loading="lazy"
            width="1200"
            height="675"
          />
          <figcaption>
            Enabling remove-after-spin prevents duplicate wins cleanly across multi-prize raffle rounds.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h2 id="multiple-prizes">6. Managing More Than One Prize</h2>

        <h3>Option A: One wheel, several spins (simplest)</h3>
        <p>
          Turn on remove-after-spin and draw smallest prize to biggest from the
          same wheel. The pool shrinks with each spin, which naturally raises
          the tension as you approach the final prize.
        </p>
        <h3>Option B: A separate wheel per prize group</h3>
        <p>
          Useful if different year groups have different prize pools — build and
          bookmark a wheel for each so you can switch between them without
          rebuilding anything mid-event.
        </p>
        <h3>Option C: Name wheel + prize wheel combo</h3>
        <p>
          Spin one wheel to pick a student, then a second to assign them a
          random prize from a roughly equal set. Works well at classroom level —
          see our
          <a href="/blogs/best-uses-random-wheel-spinner" class="ilink"
            >guide to creative uses of a random wheel</a
          >
          for more on this format.
        </p>

