        <h2 id="tips">8. Lessons From Schools That Do This Every Term</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Verify your entry count before the draw.</strong> Count
            confirmed entrants, count wheel entries, make sure they match, and
            say the total out loud before spinning.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong
              >Use first name + initial for anything recorded or shared.</strong
            >
            "Emma T." rather than a full name protects privacy in a video or
            social post while staying identifiable in the room.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Only enter ticket numbers that were actually sold.</strong>
            Not your full print run — keep a sold-ticket ledger to check the
            winning number against.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Have an independent colleague check the entry list.</strong>
            A two-minute check against the original register heads off any later
            dispute.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Tell students the rules in advance.</strong> A quick
            assembly mention the week before builds anticipation and sets
            expectations.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Record every draw as a habit.</strong> "Here's the video"
            beats "trust us" every time a question comes up later.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Build and test the wheel the night before.</strong> Not five
            minutes before assembly — technical hiccups in front of 300 students
            are avoidable.
          </li>
        </ul>

