        <h2 id="faq">9. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            Can parents verify the draw was fair?<span class="faq-icon">+</span>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            Yes — run it live in front of an audience, record it, share the
            wheel link beforehand so the entry list can be checked
            independently, and have a staff member not involved in organising
            verify the entries first. Any combination of these gives parents
            something concrete rather than just reassurance.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            What stops the same student winning twice?<span class="faq-icon"
              >+</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            Turn on "remove after spin" before your first draw. Each winning
            entry is automatically taken off the wheel the moment it's drawn, so
            a repeat win in the same session isn't possible. Show the audience
            the name disappearing before you spin again.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            How many entries can the wheel hold?<span class="faq-icon">+</span>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            Up to 500 entries load cleanly per wheel—plenty for a class of 25 or a mid-sized PTA ticket pool. If a whole-school fundraiser sells more than 500 tickets, use Bulk Paste in numbered 500-ticket batches (first spin selects the batch, second spin picks the winning ticket inside that batch) to keep every slice crisp and legible.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            Does clicking the spin button affect the result?<span
              class="faq-icon"
              >+</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            No. The outcome is set the instant the spin starts, before the
            animation plays — the timing of the click makes no difference.
            That's exactly why it's worth letting a student or parent do the
            clicking: it visibly proves the operator has no control over where
            it lands.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            What if a winning student is absent on draw day?<span
              class="faq-icon"
              >+</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            Decide this before the draw and announce it in advance — either
            their entry stays in and they collect the prize on return, or absent
            students are excluded from that day's draw. Either rule is fine as
            long as it's set and communicated ahead of time, not decided on the
            spot.
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="conclusion">10. Conclusion</h2>
        <p>
          A school raffle should be simple to run and fun to watch — and the
          draw itself is where that either holds together or falls apart. A
          projected spin wheel makes the process visible to everyone, keeps the
          result provably random, and leaves you with a recording if anyone ever
          asks how it went. The hat-and-tickets method got schools this far, but
          once a hall full of students has watched a wheel tick down to a
          winner, it's hard to go back.
        </p>
